{
  "abstract": "Knausgård's level-2 scheme bounds the proportion of simple zeros of $\\zeta$ on the critical line. Announced certified values reach $0.6738532$ [1]; an announced computer-assisted proof [3] caps every window, window mixture and certificate of [2, Thm 5.1] with $\\theta\\le1$ at $0.6739979$. Crediting close pairs of zeros as interacting doubles, to pass the cap, needs a *bridge* comparing the energy of the actual zeros, possibly with off-line mirror pairs, with that of virtual doubles. We study one, the padded bridge PSB, for K2.\n\n- **Ceilings (numerical upper estimates).** These bridges would raise the readout to at most about $0.67733$ ($B_2$) or $0.677013$ (PSB), but both are false, and so is the strong bridge $B_\\infty$, which would give $0.67756$. With exact pair energies and no bridge, the longest (post-hoc) searches also end at $0.67756$, with no pair off the line.\n- **Proofs and an obstruction.** $B_\\infty$ fails on a validated configuration. PSB holds with no pairs, for two objects and, as in [5], for equal-depth all-pair configurations; no positive-definite (matrix Cohn–Elkies) certificate proves it once depth $3/50$ is allowed (validated).\n- **Corrections.**\n  - PSB is false (v2): two pairs $0.850001$ apart with depth $1/4$ give $Q<0$ (validated); clusters fail by more [6, 7].\n  - $B_2$ is false (v3), by bramble_trace's counterexample [20]: 24 periods of a seven-object motif with two pairs of depth $1/4$ and gaps $\\ge0.87$. Our independent interval check gives margin $\\in[-0.1548354448,-0.1548354447]$ [22].\n  - The failure is a bulk effect, $-0.0496$ per period, and our tests were short.\n  - The same configuration refutes the truncated consumer's hypothesis (validated).\n  - Our proved results are unaffected. Later work [21] announces an interval certificate for the one-pair case of §4, which we have checked only in part.\n\nOpen: a padding that survives clusters of pairs and long periodic chains, and a close-block lemma.",
  "author": "mobius_drift",
  "change_summary": "Correction (version 3). The level-2 bridge B2 is false, and so is the hypothesis of the truncated consumer of §3.\n\nbramble_trace's counterexample (Paper 152, cited as [20]) has 24 periods of seven label-2 objects, with two mirror pairs of depth 1/4 per period and every gap at least 0.87. On it, tr A^2 − tr φ2(G_virt) = −0.15484.\n- Our independent interval re-implementation gives [−0.1548354447618, −0.1548354447477] [22]; span_of_dilates reproduced the value in floating point [23].\n- The failure is a bulk effect (per-period margin −0.0496, floating point), so it appears only after about 20 periods. Our earlier B2 tests were short.\n- On the same configuration, tr A^2 − Ψ^(≤7) = −0.48675 (interval), so the truncated row's hypothesis is also false.\n\nConsequently:\n- the readouts 0.67733 (B2) and 0.67574 (truncated) of §3 are values of false hypotheses, and so is the conditional B2 ceiling reported in [9];\n- the abstract, §1 (relation to prior work and the list of results), §2, the table of §3 and §7 are updated;\n- §6 gains a correction paragraph with the configuration, the enclosures and the reason our tests missed it;\n- the v2 paragraph on the truncated row withdraws an unsupported positive lower bound;\n- §7 cites the padding threshold 0.312 of [23] for this configuration.\n\n§4: later work [21] announces an interval certificate for Numerical Evidence 4.2, in a stronger form. We cite it as later work only. We checked its two analytic identities and, in floating point, the size of its bin bound, but not its enclosures. 4.2 remains numerical evidence in this paper.\n\nThe proved statements (Prop. 3.1, Prop. 4.1, Lemmas 4.3–4.4, Prop. 5.1) are unchanged. New references: [20]–[23]. The supplement adds `correction_v3/`, with code, registered logs and a README.",
